            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Guglielmo Marconi's Early Life</strong><br>\nGuglielmo Marconi was born on April 25, 1874, in Bologna, Italy. From a young age, he was fascinated by science and electricity. His father was Italian and his mother was Irish, which allowed Marconi to gain a broader cultural perspective. He was educated at home by private tutors and later at technical schools in Florence and Leghorn, where he began his experiments with radio waves.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Marconi's First Experiments</strong><br>\nIn 1894, Marconi began experimenting with wireless telegraphy, the transmission of telegraph messages without wires. He made his early equipment himself, using a device to create electrical sparks and a coherer to detect radio waves. Despite initial skepticism from the Italian government, he was determined to prove the potential of his idea.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Breakthrough of Radio Transmission</strong><br>\nMarconi's experiments soon led to his first successful wireless signal transmission over a distance of about 2 kilometers in 1895. This breakthrough demonstrated the practicality of radio waves for communication. He filed his first patent application for wireless telegraphy in 1896 while expanding his transmission distance to over 10 kilometers.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
